sections.content:
**State machine.** A Promise exposes no operation that moves it back to pending. Settlement is permanent, which lets many consumers observe the same eventual outcome without racing to replace it.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  P[Pending] -->|resolve with value| F[Fulfilled · settled]
  P -->|reject or thrown error| R[Rejected · settled]
```

**Runnable ordering example.** The executor prints immediately, while the `then` callback waits until the synchronous stack has completed.

```javascript
console.log("start");
const result = new Promise((resolve) => {
  console.log("executor");
  resolve(42);
  resolve(99); // ignored
});
result.then((value) => console.log("fulfilled", value));
console.log("end");
// start, executor, end, fulfilled 42
```

**Boundary.** Promise state is not cancellation state. Cancelling underlying work needs an API-specific mechanism such as `AbortSignal`; rejecting a wrapper alone does not stop work already running elsewhere.

support.code:
const doubleLater = number => new Promise(resolve => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(number * 2), 5);
});

Promise.resolve(3)
  .then(number => doubleLater(number))
  .then(number => {
    console.log(number); // 6
    return number + 1;
  })
  .then(number => console.log(number)); // 7

sections.content:
**Resolution rule.** The new Promise from `then` waits for the handler to run. Its result is processed: values fulfill, thrown exceptions reject, and thenables are assimilated. This rule turns a sequence of independent callbacks into one observable pipeline.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  P[Original Promise] --> T[then handler]
  T -->|return value| V[Next Promise fulfilled]
  T -->|return Promise| W[Next Promise adopts it]
  T -->|throw| R[Next Promise rejected]
  T -->|no return| U[Next Promise gets undefined]
```

**Runnable example.** The returned delay keeps `step 2` behind the asynchronous work and passes its value forward.

```javascript
const delayValue = (value) => new Promise((resolve) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(value), 5);
});

await Promise.resolve(3)
  .then((number) => delayValue(number * 2))
  .then((number) => {
    console.log(number); // 6
    return number + 1;
  })
  .then((number) => console.log(number)); // 7
```

**Boundary.** A handler registered as `then(doWork())` is also wrong because it calls `doWork` immediately and passes its result instead of a callback. Use `then(() => doWork())` when arguments or delayed invocation are required.

